{
 "version": 1,
 "source": {
  "title": "Region of Peel – 2020 Water and Wastewater Master Plan for the Lake-Based Systems, Volume 3 (Water)",
  "url": "https://peelregion.ca/sites/default/files/2024-07/ea-peel-2020-master-plan-volume-3.pdf",
  "published": "June 2020",
  "basis": "2020 Master Plan basis. Peel launched a Master Plan update in 2025, so these figures may be superseded."
 },
 "notes": [
  "The lake-based system has three trunk systems: West (Lorne Park WTP), Central (A.P. Kennedy WTP) and East (fed from Central via the Beckett Sproule transfer). Vol 3, p.14.",
  "Each lake-based pressure zone is split into W, C and E sub-zones (1W, 1C, ... 7E); Bolton is 6B. The plan gives demand by sub-zone but does not publish sub-zone boundary maps (\"approximate service area boundaries\", p.34).",
  "West and Central/East are interconnected, so water can be moved between them to optimize capacity (p.35). Treat sub-zone checks as per-system screening, not hard limits.",
  "Actual capacity uses roughness from the calibrated hydraulic model; theoretical uses new-pipe roughness. Both at a 1 m/km head-loss gradient (pp.19, 134).",
  "York Region supply is treated at A.P. Kennedy, pumped north through the Central system (A.P. Kennedy–Hanlan, CT2H; Hanlan–Beckett Sproule, CT3) and transferred east at Beckett Sproule to Airport Road (p.64). Those two trunks carry \"carriesYork\": true; their flow includes the York agreement amounts in systemDemand.york."
 ],
 "years": [
  2021,
  2026,
  2031,
  2036,
  2041
 ],
